874:we showed that instruction files and configuration tabs are the primary invisible budget consumers in typical coding sessions, \hl{contributing approximately 21\% of total context usage in our illustrative example}, and that real-time decomposition into five cost categories makes this immediately actionable. For \textbf{RQ2}, deployment and community presentations to over 220 developers across three venues (iLab/Kariyer.net, SaaS Bridge, TechCareer) showed that the five-signal syntactic relevance scorer correctly flags low-relevance tabs (such as configuration files and unrelated language files) without false positives. %% RISKY CLAIM: "All participants agreed" — same issue as in §4.2: universal claim from
877:\hl{Participants consistently agreed that the flagged distractors were appropriate candidates for removal.} For \textbf{RQ3}, closed-form economic models for caching break-even ($n^* = 2$ for Anthropic), conversation cost strategies ($O(T^2)$ vs.\ $O(T)$), and Cobb–Douglas quality optimization are validated by 124 unit tests and packaged into interactive calculators that make complex decisions, such as when to cache, which conversation strategy to use, and how to allocate tokens, accessible without manual calculations. %% UPDATE [v3.1.2]: Replace v3.1.1 → v3.1.2, and "15 models" → "14 models".
883:Future work priorities, grounded in the survey findings (Appendix~\ref{app:survey}) and recent literature, are as follows.
885:\textbf{(1)~Controlled user study.} The qualitative survey ($n=50$, Appendix~\ref{app:survey}) provides hypothesis-generating evidence but lacks a control group. A randomized controlled experiment with pre/post API-cost measurement is needed to quantify the causal effect of context budget visibility on developer productivity and spend. The Adaptive Context Management (ACM) framework of Perera et al.~\cite{perera2025acm} provides a formal evaluation baseline for conversational context optimization that could be adapted for IDE-level measurement.
891:\textbf{(4)~Compaction decomposition.} Survey Theme~5 (48\% of respondents) identified the inability to distinguish file-token growth from conversation-history growth as the primary gap in \texttt{/compaction} output. The ACM framework~\cite{perera2025acm} provides a modular design (Context Manager, Summarization, and Entity Extraction modules) that maps directly onto this decomposition: Tokalator could expose separate \textit{file-context} and \textit{conversation-history} growth curves in the \texttt{/compaction} report, each with its own projected compaction point.
893:\textbf{(5)~Multi-lingual tokenizer accuracy.} Survey Theme~2 revealed 15--32\% underestimation errors for Turkish and Arabic content under the 4-chars/token heuristic. Integrating Google's server-side \texttt{countTokens} API (with a fallback cache) would eliminate the heuristic for Gemini models. For multilingual coding teams, cross-lingual prompt optimization strategies~\cite{gupta2025crosslingual} (arXiv:2507.22923) suggest that token efficiency varies substantially with language and query strategy — a dimension Tokalator's calculator does not currently model.
895:\textbf{(6)~Session history dashboard.} Survey Theme~4 (38\% of respondents) requested persistent cross-session token history. Storing session summaries in VS~Code's \texttt{globalState} (rather than \texttt{workspaceState}) would allow cross-project comparison. A lightweight SQLite log could expose per-day peak usage, average turn cost, and model distribution in a new ``History'' tab in the sidebar dashboard.
899:\textbf{(8)~Tokalator Pro: enterprise-grade context governance.} The community survey and marketplace adoption data indicate demand beyond individual developers. A planned \textit{Tokalator Pro} tier would add four enterprise capabilities: (a)~\textit{team dashboard} --- shared context budget visibility across an organisation, aggregating per-developer and per-project token consumption into a centralised view; (b)~\textit{historical analytics} --- persistent token usage logs queryable by day, sprint, model, and project, enabling spend forecasting and anomaly detection (complementing the session-history request from survey Theme~4); (c)~\textit{CI/CD budget gates} --- a configurable threshold check that fails pull-request builds when projected context consumption exceeds a policy limit, bringing context governance into the software delivery pipeline; and (d)~\textit{custom model profiles} --- a plugin mechanism for internal or fine-tuned models not in the default 14-model catalogue, with user-supplied BPE vocabulary files and pricing overrides. The open-source core remains MIT-licensed; Pro features would be delivered as a separately licensed server component with a local VS~Code panel.
