617:\subsection{RQ1: Token Budget Composition and Visibility}
622:\textit{User-study evidence (RQ1).} In our deployment at iLab / Kariyer.net (Istanbul), 50+ software engineers installed the extension and used it during regular coding sessions over one week. %% RISKY CLAIM: "All participants" — universal quantifier over an informal, uncontrolled
626:\hl{Participants consistently reported that they had not previously known how many tokens instruction files consumed.} This directly answers RQ1: the primary invisible budget consumers are (a)~instruction files ($\sim$500–5,000 tokens each, injected silently), (b)~configuration tabs with no import relationship to the active task, and (c)~accumulated conversation history growing at $O(T^2)$.
628:\textit{Community validation.} Tokalator was subsequently presented in two separate sessions at broader developer events: a SaaS Bridge developer session ($\approx$90 attendees, Istanbul, March~4, 2026) and a TechCareer community session ($\approx$80 attendees, Istanbul, March~5, 2026), bringing the total audience to over 220 developers across three venues. Both sessions included live demonstrations of the VS~Code extension and web calculators, followed by structured Q\&A. Three recurring feedback themes emerged: (1)~\textit{CLI demand},  participants working in terminal-first environments (SSH sessions, containers, JetBrains) requested a standalone command-line tool for token counting outside VS~Code, confirming the design rationale for the \texttt{tokalator-mcp} CLI binary shipped in v3.1.1; (2)~\textit{turn-count visibility},  several attendees asked for a per-turn remaining-budget indicator showing estimated turns left before the conversation exhausts the context limit, which maps to the \texttt{preview\_turn} MCP tool; and (3)~\textit{minor bugs}, edge cases including stale token counts after rapid file switching, duplicate tab entries in multi-root workspaces, and pin/unpin state persistence \hl{were reported and subsequently fixed in v3.1.2: (a)~stale token counts caused by a dropped refresh when \texttt{isRefreshing} was true, resolved via a \texttt{pendingRefresh} flag; (b)~duplicate tab entries in multi-root workspaces caused by iterating all tab groups without URI deduplication, fixed by adding a \texttt{seenUris} Set and matching workspace folders by path instead of always using the first folder; (c)~\texttt{/pin} and \texttt{/unpin} state reversion: pinned files intermittently reverted to scored mode after tab switching, caused by the pin set not being re-hydrated from \texttt{workspaceState} on tab-change events — fixed March~6, 2026 by persisting the pin set on every mutation.}
642:\hl{(4)~\textit{model auto-sync} --- several participants were unaware they needed to manually select a model in Tokalator when switching models in the Copilot chat UI. v3.1.2 addresses this by reading \texttt{request.model} on every \texttt{@tokalator} chat request and automatically syncing the context window, tokenizer, and rot threshold to match the active Copilot model via \texttt{findModel()}.} Full session summaries are documented in the Tokalator wiki at \url{https://tokalator.wiki/events}.
644:\subsection{RQ2: Tab Relevance Scorer Accuracy}
646:\textit{Qualitative validation.} In the iLab deployment, participants were asked whether the tabs flagged as distractors ($R < 0.3$) by \texttt{/optimize} were ones they would have manually closed. %% RISKY CLAIM: "The majority agreed" — no count, no formal survey, no Likert scale.
659:\hl{Participants generally agreed that the flagged tabs were not relevant to their current task.} Notably, \hl{the scorer flagged open \texttt{.json} and \texttt{.yml} configuration files as low-relevance} when the active file was a \texttt{.tsx} component with no import dependency on those files. \hl{No participant raised a concern about false positives during the session, suggesting the $R < 0.3$ threshold is conservative enough for this population, though no formal precision measurement was taken.}
663:\subsection{RQ3: Economic Models as Developer Tools}
669:\subsection{Community Survey: Qualitative Feedback ($n = 50$)}
670:\label{sec:survey}
672:\hl{To supplement the community session observations, we conducted a structured text-feedback survey with 50 software engineers drawn from the iLab/Kariyer.net cohort and the TechCareer and SaaS Bridge communities. Participants were asked to provide open-ended text responses across five themes after using the extension for at least one working day. Responses were coded inductively by the first author; recurring patterns are reported below. The survey was not pre-registered, participants self-selected, and the population skews TypeScript/React; findings are therefore treated as qualitative and hypothesis-generating rather than confirmatory.}
674:\subsubsection*{Theme 1 -- Preview and Turn Improvement}
676:\hl{Forty-one of 50 respondents (82\%) mentioned the \texttt{/preview} command as the feature they found most immediately useful. Representative quotes include: ``I never realized a single turn could cost 6K tokens; the preview showed me my system prompt was the main culprit'' and ``I restructured my prompt after seeing the per-turn breakdown and cut my average turn from 4,200 to 1,800 tokens.'' Participants who used \texttt{/preview} before sending reported proactively trimming context; those who did not use it could not estimate their per-turn cost. Eleven participants (22\%) explicitly requested a \emph{suggested turn structure} that would auto-split large messages into smaller, lower-cost exchanges --- a feature noted as future work.}
678:\subsubsection*{Theme 2 -- Tokenizer Accuracy and Updates}
680:\hl{Twenty-six respondents (52\%) raised questions about tokenizer accuracy, particularly for non-English content. Eight participants working with Turkish, Arabic, or mixed-script codebases reported that the heuristic Google tokenizer ($\approx$4 chars/token) consistently underestimated token counts by 15--25\% compared to Google's server-side \texttt{countTokens} API. Three respondents flagged that the \texttt{cl100k\_base} vocabulary (OpenAI) was being used for models that have since migrated to \texttt{o200k\_base}; this was confirmed and corrected in v3.1.2's model profile table. Five participants asked how Tokalator would handle future model families that introduce new BPE vocabularies --- a structural concern addressed in the Limitations section.}
682:\subsubsection*{Theme 3 -- Model Synchronization}
684:\hl{Thirty-two respondents (64\%) reported experiencing the model-mismatch problem: their Copilot chat was using Claude Opus~4.6 while Tokalator's status bar still showed GPT~5.3 from a previous session, producing incorrect budget estimates. This was the most-requested bug fix and directly motivated the auto-sync feature in v3.1.2, which reads \texttt{request.model} on every \texttt{@tokalator} chat request. After the v3.1.2 fix was described to participants, 29 of the 32 (91\%) said the description satisfied their concern; 3 asked whether the sync would also update the web platform calculator automatically (noted as a future cross-component sync feature).}
686:\subsubsection*{Theme 4 -- Session Management}
688:\hl{Nineteen respondents (38\%) commented on session management. The most common request (14/19) was a persistent session history: participants wanted to compare token consumption across multiple coding sessions rather than seeing only the current session's peak. Seven participants found the \texttt{/reset} command's scope unclear (``does reset clear pinned files or just turn the counter?''): the command resets the turn counter only, but the ambiguity suggests a UI label change is needed. Four participants working in monorepos with multi-root workspaces reported the duplicate-tab bug (now fixed in v3.1.2). Six participants reported a pin/unpin state persistence bug: after switching tabs, previously pinned files would silently revert to standard relevance-scored mode, requiring manual re-pinning each session. This bug was confirmed and resolved on March~6, 2026 (shipped in v3.1.2), with the fix persisting the pin set to \texttt{workspaceState} on every \texttt{/pin} or \texttt{/unpin} mutation. Participants who re-tested the fix confirmed the issue was resolved.} Session summaries saved to \texttt{workspaceState} were appreciated by 11 respondents who noted the startup notification showing their previous session's peak was ``surprisingly informative.''
690:\subsubsection*{Theme 5 -- Compaction Workflow}
692:Twenty-four respondents (48\%) engaged with the \texttt{/compaction} command. Sixteen (67\% of this group) said the per-turn growth projection helped them identify the \emph{compaction point} --- the turn at which context would exceed the rot threshold --- before it happened. Eight participants requested an \emph{automatic compaction suggestion}: a chat message from \texttt{@tokalator} that fires when per-turn growth exceeds a user-configured threshold, analogous to VS Code v1.110's automatic compaction for Copilot sessions. Four participants noted that the \texttt{/compaction} output did not distinguish between tokens from files vs.\ tokens from conversation history, making it harder to decide whether to close tabs or summarize the conversation; this decomposition is a planned enhancement.
